"PeopleSoft EPM 9.1: Global (https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E41508_01/psft/acrobat/epm91pgcs-b0613.pdf)\nciteturn9729search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.2 years ago; Note: You subtract one from the record suites assigned because one of the assigned record suites is ... balancing: Minimum Number of Ledger Business Units and Maximum Number of Load Cycles.\npages for the ledger business units that you permit them to access. Row-level security is set up within\nPeopleSoft EPM, but you must enable it within PeopleSoft Global Consolidations in order to use it. It is\nrecommended that you select no more than two fields on which to enforce row-level security, or system\nperformance may degrade.\nYou can also specify that the row-level security you set up to limit access also be extended to Global\nConsolidations SQR reports.\nNote: You can also use PeopleTools security to limit access to menus or pages.\nLoad Balancing Options\nTo optimize performance during ledger preparation processing, the system performs load balancing\nto control the number of ledger business units that are processed concurrently using the Spawn\n(PF_SPAWN) application engine.PF_SPAWN. Load balancing is based on the total number of ledger\nbusiness units, the number of record suites that are assigned, the size of your temporary tables, and the\nmaximum number of load cycles.\nThe formula used to determine how many concurrent ledger business units are processed is:\n(total number of ledger business units) ÷ ((record suites assigned) – 1)\nNote: You subtract one from the record suites assigned because one of the assigned record suites is\nalways used as the controlling record suite; this reduces the number of available records suites by one.\nThere are two fields on the General Options page in which you can specify factors that impact load\nbalancing: Minimum Number of Ledger Business Units and Maximum Number of Load Cycles. If\n108\n Copyright © 1999, 2013, Oracle and/or its affiliates. All rights reserved.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nK-Pile WythoﬀGames (https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~fraenkel/Papers/WythoffN_DavidAviezri.pdf)\nciteturn9729search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; leaving the other K −n piles untouched, those n piles will all have size zero ... Given n+1 piles, let s be the smallest pile size and let i be\nWe note that in Lythoﬀ(K) with K > 2 any position T with at least two\nnon-empty piles has an inﬁnite number of sub-positions and there is no upper\nbound on the number of moves till the game ends. This is true because if\nthe two piles have sizes 0 < a ≤b then we can choose the two integers 1 and\n−n < 0 to be elements of S so the move a →a −1, b →b + n is legal for all\nn > 0. We therefore need the following theorem:\nTheorem 1. Lythoﬀ(K) always ends after a ﬁnite number of moves.\nProof. Consider Lythoﬀ(K) for some ﬁxed value K. We will prove by induc-\ntion on n that when both players limit their moves to a ﬁxed set of n piles,\nleaving the other K −n piles untouched, those n piles will all have size zero\nafter a ﬁnite number of moves. The case n = K then proves the theorem.\nFor n = 1 it is obvious that since all other piles are unchanged, each\nmove must decrease the size of the single pile, which can only happen a ﬁnite\nnumber of times. Given n+1 piles, let s be the smallest pile size and let i be\na pile whose size is s. If we leave pile i untouched then we are making moves\non a ﬁxed set of n piles. By the induction hypothesis after a ﬁnite number\nof moves all their sizes will be zero. At that point we will have to reduce the\ni’th pile. Whether we do this only at the end or at some intermediate step\nin either case the size of the smallest pile among the n + 1 has been reduced.\nSince this can only happen at most s times, all n + 1 piles will have size zero\nin a ﬁnite number of steps, thus proving the induction.\nOne might wonder why we consider Lythoﬀ(K) a natural generalization of\nWythoﬀ. We have already shown that Lythoﬀ(2) is indeed the usual Wythoﬀ\ngame, but in view of the fact that for K > 2 the K-Pile game isn’t even a\nshort game (a short game may only have a ﬁnite number of sub-positions)\nand thus isn’t even in the same class as the Wythoﬀgame, in what sense is it\na natural extension? Isn’t the K-Pile game deﬁned in section 5, Problem 11\nof [3] more natural? The reason we feel our current deﬁnition best retains the\nﬂavor of the original Wythoﬀgame is the fact that, as in the original game,\nthe pile sizes of its P-Positions form a disjoint cover of the natural numbers.\nWe prove this statement in the next section. To the best of our knowledge\nthis does not occur for any other generalizations to K piles.\n5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nICOMEU 2022EditedBookFinal (https://www.icomeu.gr/ICOMEU%202022EditedBookFinal.pdf)\nciteturn9729search13 [wordlim: 200] In case the pile, in which the robot is located is the smallest of all other piles (sorted item), then the robot simply moves to the next pile.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e4\">\nFigure 6a. Starting position: Street/Avenue grid with the robot at the base of the pile near Avenue 3, Street 1.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e5\">\nFigure 6b. Counting: Street/Avenue grid with the robot at the top of the middle pile and beeper counts shown in circles.\n</visual_element>\n\n<visual_element id=\"e6\">\nFigure 6c. Preparing for sorting: Street/Avenue grid with the robot at the base of the left pile and beeper counts shown in circles.\n</visual_element>\n\nUsing the selection method and based on the data listed in the array, Genius identifies each time which pile has fewer beepers from where it is located and to the end of the piles, leaves in the located pile only those beepers contained in the smallest pile and collects the remaining (Figure 7A). Subsequently, it moves to the location of the smallest pile, rises to the top of it, and unload all the beepers which carries with it (Figure 7B). Finally, the robot moves into the base of the pile and returns to the next position from the pile that was standing to repeat the above process until the end (figure 7c). The program also makes the necessary swap of the corresponding two values of the array. In case the pile, in which the robot is located is the smallest of all other piles (sorted item), then the robot simply moves to the next pile.\n\n294--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEstablishing Consolidation Options and Supporting Objects (https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E13110_01/epm9pbr1/eng/psbooks/pgcs/htm/pgcs06.htm)\nciteturn9729search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; By default the system performs general load balancing when you set both the minimum number of ledger business units and the maximum number of load cycles to one.\n\nThe formula used to determine how many concurrent ledger business units are processed is:\n\n(total number of ledger business units) ÷ ((record suites assigned) – 1)\n\nNote. You subtract one from the record suites assigned because one of the assigned record suites is always used as the controlling record suite; this reduces the number of available records suites by one.\n\nThere are two fields on the General Options page in which you can specify factors that impact load balancing: Minimum Number of Ledger Business Units and Maximum Number of Load Cycles. If the processing run contains only a few ledger business units, there is no real benefit gained by using PF_SPAWN and processing concurrently. Therefore, if the result of the concurrent ledger business units calculation is less than the minimum number of ledger business units, the system processes everything in sequence. However, when the result of the concurrent ledger business units calculation is greater than the minimum number of ledger business units, the resulting amount is the number that the system processes concurrently, with the size of the chunk equal to that number divided by the maximum number of load cycles.\n\nFor example, suppose that you have 2,000 ledger business units to process, and 10 record suites allocated to ledger preparation. The Minimum Number of Ledger Business Units = 20. The Maximum Number of Load Cycles = 5 . Applying the formula to determine the number of concurrent ledger business units processed, calculates to 2000 ÷ (10 – 1) = 222.22 , or 223 when rounded up to the nearest whole number. Because 223 is greater than 20 (the minimum number of ledger business units to process), concurrent processing occurs. The system includes 223/5 (the maximum number of load cycles), or, when rounded, 45 ledger business units within each chunk.\n\nBy default the system performs general load balancing when you set both the minimum number of ledger business units and the maximum number of load cycles to one. This means that the system balances across the available record suites.\n\nSee Streamlining Processing with Jobstreams.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science (https://dmtcs.episciences.org/4214/pdf)\nciteturn9729search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.6 years ago; is not possible for a vertex to have a negative number of chips, the dynamics for parallel chip-ﬁring on ... the process as a redistribution of chips on a graph no longer holds. ... We call the minimum such p the period length\nWe now formally deﬁne the diffusion game. Let G be a graph with V (G) = {v1, v2, . . . , vn}. A chip\nconﬁguration on G is a vector ct = (ct(v1), ct(v2), . . . , ct(vn)) ∈Zn, where c0(vi) denotes the number\nof chips at vertex vi in the initial conﬁguration and ct(vi) ∈Z is the number of chips on vertex vi at step t.\nWe deﬁne the parallel dynamics as\nct+1(vi) = ct(vi) −\n\f\f\f{vj ∈N(vi) : ct(vi) > ct(vj)}\n\f\f\f +\n\f\f\f{vj ∈N(vi) : ct(vi) < ct(vj)}\n\f\f\f.\n(1)\nWe refer to this dynamic as ﬁring. We note that Equation (1) is applied synchronously on V (G). It is\nimportant to observe that P\nv∈V (G) c0(v) = P\nv∈V (G) ci(v) for all i ≥0. We further observe that the\ndynamics are unchanged if an additional constant number of chips is added to each vertex. As such we\nmay assume without loss of generality that P\nv∈V (G) ci(v) = C ≥0 for all i ≥0.\nFor neighbours u, v ∈V (G) such that ck−1(u) > ck−1(v) we say that in the kth ﬁring u sends a\nchip to v and v receives a chip from u. When a chip conﬁguration has negative entries the analogy of\nthe process as a redistribution of chips on a graph no longer holds. In this case we may consider a chip\nconﬁguration to be a function that assigns an integer value to each vertex. Alternatively we may consider\nentries of the chip conﬁguration to model charge as in Cranston and West (2016). The application of\nthe parallel dynamics in Equation (1) does not depend on the interpretation of chips as physical objects.\nHowever, if the parallel dynamics necessarily lead to a periodic sequence of chip conﬁgurations, we may\nadd a constant so that all ci(v) ≥0 for all v ∈V and all i ≥0 to restore the analogy. It is unknown,\nhowever, if the parallel dynamics always lead to a periodic process.\nLet c0 be an initial chip conﬁguration on some graph G. We observe that if for some p ∈N, ct = ct+p,\nthen the dynamics imply ct+q = ct+p+q for any q ∈N. We call the minimum such p the period length\nand given p, we call the minimum such t the length of the pre-period. In this case, we say that c0 exhibits\neventual periodic behaviour with period length p and pre-period length t.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGROMACS Reference Manual (https://manuals.plus/m/3f68561a919ad12933cf26cdbae25e71b8241b14c39e9fe7607e12a8064ca302)\nciteturn9729search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;     There is one important parameter for the dynamic load balancing, which is the minimum allowed scaling.\nNote that the reported force load imbalance numbers might be higher, since the force calculation is only part of work that needs to be done during an integration step. The load imbalance is reported in the log file at log output steps and when the -v option is used also on screen. The average load imbalance and the total performance loss due to load imbalance are reported at the end of the log file.\n    There is one important parameter for the dynamic load balancing, which is the minimum allowed scaling. By default, each dimension of the domain decomposition cell can scale down by at least a factor of 0.8. For 3-D domain decomposition this allows cells to change their volume by about a factor of 0.5, which should allow for compensation of a load imbalance of 100%. The minimum allowed scaling can be changed with the -dds option of mdrun.\n    The load imbalance is measured by timing a single region of the MD step on each MPI rank. This region can not include MPI communication, as timing of MPI calls does not allow separating wait due to imbalance from actual communication. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTriple Klondike Turn 3 – Play Free Online – TheSolitaire (https://thesolitaire.com/triple-klondike-turn-3)\nciteturn9729search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Build up each home pile by suit, from A to K (A, 2, 3, …). ... One of these is an exciting triple-deck variant called Turn-Three (Triple) Solitaire. ... The main play area has 13 columns, with the first containing one card, the second card containing two cards, the third containing three cards, the fourth containing four cards and so on, with the 13th column containing 13 cards. ... For instance, try to empty the smallest pile in the Tableau first if you have a King to place there.\n\nDiscover our Turn-Three (Triple) Klondike Solitaire zone, where you can delve into a diverse collection of Solitaire games designed to captivate players of all levels!\n\n## Are you ready to explore?\n\n## Playing Turn-Three (Triple) Klondike Solitaire — The Layout\n\nTurn-Three (Triple) Klondike Solitaire is a variant that stands out in Solitaire, as it lets you see three cards from the Stockpile at once. More than that, using three decks reflects thrice the volume of the game’s layout, from the Tableau to the Foundation piles.\nThere are four main areas in the Klondike Solitaire layout:\n\n  * ### 1.The Tableau\n\nThe main play area has 13 columns, with the first containing one card, the second card containing two cards, the third containing three cards, the fourth containing four cards and so on, with the 13th column containing 13 cards. Only the topmost card of each column is face-up.\n\nImage: tableau Turn-3 Triple Klondike\n\n  * ### 2.The Stockpile\n\nThe Stockpile is where the remaining 65 cards go after laying out the Tableau as described above. Specifically, the number of cards you draw depends on the Klondike Solitaire variant. In the Turn-1 version, you draw one card at a time. In the Turn-3 version, you draw three cards, but only the top card can be played at any point.\n\nImage: stockpile Turn-3 Triple Klondike\n\n  * ### 3.The Waste Pile\n\nWhen cards are drawn from the Stockpile, they go to the Wastepile, making them available for play on the Tableau. Of course, you could also choose to draw from the Stockpile once more. Ultimately, once the Stockpile is empty, the Wastepile is reshuffled to reinstate a new Stockpile.\n\nImage: waste pile Turn-3 Triple Klondike\n\n  * ### 4.The Foundation\n\nThere are 12 Foundation piles that you’ll build in ascending order for each suit, from Ace to King.\n\nImage: foundation Turn-3 Triple Klondike\n\nIt’s easy to draw cards from the Stockpile without a strategy, but this usually makes the entire layout more chaotic and hard to untangle. After all, the Tableau has 91 cards before you even start adding to it! Placing too many cards from the Stockpile can cause your important cards, such as Kings, to be stuck behind unnecessary cards. So try not to do this, as it can create blockages that are challenging to navigate through.\n\n  * ### Focus On Revealing Cards First\n\nYou need to prioritize revealing cards so you can open up more important cards, like Aces. Instead of focusing on just making sequences, try to make moves that will uncover more cards. This will help you play the game without depending on just luck.  For instance, try to empty the smallest pile in the Tableau first if you have a King to place there. Another trick is to make shorter sequences first, which will help you reveal more cards and open up more moves. Uncovering cards can help you plan your moves ahead of time and not leave everything up to luck. \n\n  * ### Fun Fact!\n\nIn Turn-Three Triple Klondike, the use of three decks yields over 1,500 distinct card combinations, increasing the game’s complexity.\n\n  * ### Did you know?\n\nKlondike Solitaire, often referred to as 'Solitaire' in the U.S., gained widespread popularity when Microsoft Windows featured it as a default game in the 1990s.\n\n  * ### Did you know?\n\nApart from enhancing focus and problem-solving abilities, playing Solitaire also offers a rewarding sense of achievement upon completion.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnhancing Redistribution Layers for Advanced Packaging in AI Acceleration Chips (https://eureka.patsnap.com/report-enhancing-redistribution-layers-for-advanced-packaging-in-ai-acceleration-chips)\nciteturn9729search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Current redistribution layer (RDL) technologies face significant limitations when applied to AI acceleration chip packaging, primarily stemming from the demanding requirements of high-performance computing applications. ... The photolithography limitations of existing production lines restrict minimum feature sizes, preventing the implementation of ultra-high-density interconnect patterns necessary for advanced AI chip architectures.\nContemporary RDL materials, predominantly organic substrates with limited thermal conductivity, fail to adequately dissipate the substantial heat generated by AI acceleration chips. The thermal resistance of standard RDL stacks often exceeds 0.5 K·cm²/W, resulting in junction temperatures that compromise chip performance and reliability. This thermal bottleneck becomes particularly problematic in multi-chip module configurations where heat density can exceed 200 W/cm².\nManufacturing scalability poses additional constraints. Current RDL fabrication processes struggle with the precision required for fine-pitch interconnects while maintaining acceptable yield rates. The photolithography limitations of existing production lines restrict minimum feature sizes, preventing the implementation of ultra-high-density interconnect patterns necessary for advanced AI chip architectures.\nPower delivery network inadequacies further compound these limitations. Existing RDL designs cannot efficiently support the dynamic power requirements of AI processors, which demand rapid current transients and stable voltage regulation across multiple power domains. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrategies for Restricted Nim (https://theses.liacs.nl/pdf/2018-2019-HuibersJA.pdf)\nciteturn9729search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.7 years ago; player took from the other pile, which means that the two piles will have the same number ... as that of the second smallest pile by ﬁrst removing the largest pile, this will leave two ... In all other cases the ﬁrst player will win because it is always\nto empty one of the piles will lose, as they leave their opponent in a situation with three\npiles. The situation in which a player is forced to remove a pile is when all piles have only\none chip left. In this situation there are tw